PUBLIC RESPONSE: A. Bravehearts: Men in Skirts

The case studies that Ellsworth builds her account of pedagogy’s hinge around in Places of Learning: Media, Architecture, Pedagogy are institutional / performative sites in which a threshold is mobilised to put an inside and an outside in productive relation and thereby re-work personal and social boundaries.

Those core hinge sites are

* B. U.S. Holocaust Memorial Museum – the Ramp of Remembrance and the museum’s sequence of thresholding is the book’s opening architectural hinge;
* D. Jane Addams Hull-House Museum – the house/museum as a hinge between the neighbourhood, the institution and histories of settlement work;
* F. Manhattan Children’s Museum’s Art Inside Out – the Art Inside Out project is used as the hinge that takes art and learning out of the museum and into the street and back again;
* C. Anna Deavere Smith’s performances – e.g. Twilight: Los Angeles, 1992 / Fires in the Mirror, used as a performative/verbal hinge between speaker, character and audience.

Bravehearts: Men in Skirts is not one of the Places of Learning case studies. The Chicago Symphony Orchestra’s Education Center is also not developed as a hinge case in the book; the architectural/performative hinges Ellsworth analyses are the Holocaust Museum, Hull-House, the Children’s Museum of Manhattan’s Art Inside Out and Anna Deavere Smith’s performance work.

