PUBLIC RESPONSE: E. Chicago Symphony Orchestra’s Education Center

The hinge cases Ellsworth actually develops in *Places of Learning: Media, Architecture, Pedagogy* are threshold sites that work inside/outside, public/private, self/other into relation.

The hinges repeatedly mobilised in the book are:

* B. U.S. Holocaust Memorial Museum – the Ramp of Remembrance and the threshold sequence into the Hall of Witness / Memorial Garden, the opening architectural hinge of the book;
* D. Jane Addams Hull-House Museum – the house/museum as a hinge between neighbourhood, institution and settlement history, with its porch / historic rooms re-staged as a pedagogical threshold;
* F. Manhattan Children’s Museum’s Art Inside Out – the project that literally moves art and learning out of the museum walls into the street and back again;
* C. Anna Deavere Smith’s performances – *Fires in the Mirror*, *Twilight: Los Angeles, 1992*, etc., used as a performative/verbal hinge of witnessing between speaker, character and audience;
* A. Bravehearts: Men in Skirts – discussed in the Hull-House / cultural centre context as a gender/performance intervention that stages a hinge around gendered clothing and public space.

That is the set colleagues 1,3,4,5,6,7 identify, and it matches the descriptions in the text.

E. Chicago Symphony Orchestra’s Education Center is not one of the hinge sites analysed in *Places of Learning*. It is the conventional outreach / music-education facility repeatedly named as the outlier by the majority of the group, and it lacks the architectural / performative thresholding Ellsworth is tracking.

I note Colleague 2’s dissent about Bravehearts: Men in Skirts, but the book does discuss Bravehearts as part of its Hull-House / gendered hinge material, so the single consistent non-example remains the CSO Education Center.

