E. Chicago Symphony Orchestra’s Education Center

The hinge analytic in *Places of Learning: Media, Architecture, Pedagogy* is developed through a tightly interlinked set of museum and performative sites in which a threshold is worked architecturally / performatively to put inside and outside, public and private, self and other into relation and thereby re-work boundaries.

Those sites are the ones Ellsworth returns to repeatedly in the 2005 book:

* B. U.S. Holocaust Memorial Museum – the opening architectural hinge, the Ramp of Remembrance and the sequential thresholding from city to Hall of Witness/Memorial Garden;
* D. Jane Addams Hull-House Museum – the house/museum as a hinge between neighbourhood, institutional history and settlement work, with its porch/settlement threshold;
* F. Manhattan Children’s Museum’s Art Inside Out – the project that literally moves art and learning out of the museum walls and into the street and back again;
* C. Anna Deavere Smith’s performances – *Fires in the Mirror*, *Twilight: Los Angeles, 1992*, etc., used as a performative/verbal pedagogy of witnessing and a hinge between speaker/character/audience;
* A. Bravehearts: Men in Skirts – discussed in the Hull-House / cultural centre context as a site-specific performance/installation that stages a hinge around gendered clothing and public space.

The Chicago Symphony Orchestra’s Education Center is not developed as a hinge case in the book. The other four museum/performative examples, and Bravehearts, are the hinge illustrations Ellsworth uses to theorise pedagogy’s hinge; a conventional music outreach/education program is the outlier.

