Those sites are the ones that are repeatedly developed in the book:

* B. U.S. Holocaust Memorial Museum – the Ramp of Remembrance and the museum’s sequential thresholding of memory is the opening architectural hinge;
* D. Jane Addams Hull-House Museum – the house/museum as a hinge between neighbourhood, institution and settlement history, including the reflexive re-staging of its porch/settlement threshold;
* F. Manhattan Children’s Museum’s Art Inside Out – the program that moves art and learning out of the museum walls and into the street and back again, a literal inside/outside hinge;
* C. Anna Deavere Smith’s performances – *Fires in the Mirror*, *Twilight: Los Angeles, 1992* etc. used as a performative/verbal hinge of witnessing between speaker, character and audience;
* A. Bravehearts: Men in Skirts – discussed as a site-specific performance/installation that stages a hinge around gendered clothing and public space, and is tied to the Hull-House / cultural-center discussion of pedagogical place-making.

The Chicago Symphony Orchestra’s Education Center is a conventional music outreach/education program and is not one of the architectural / performative hinge cases Ellsworth analyses in *Places of Learning*. It is the outlier in the list.

